  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 204 PM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nWednesday morning, ensembles are favoring a strong 500-mb high \nacross the Southeastern United States, with troughing to the west. \nAt the same time, a strong surface high supported by an active jet \nstream looks to be located in the Great Lakes Region. These features \nare favored to remain in place through Friday. Southerly flow would \nbe expected across the Great Plains between the southeastern high \nand western trough, however the surface high in the Great Lakes \nRegion is forecast to support easterly surface flow across Kansas. \nWarm, wet conditions look to be in place across the area Wednesday \nthrough Friday as a result of this pattern. High temperatures all \nthree days are in the upper 70s to mid 80s. It's important to note \nthat NBM 75th percentile and higher scenarios show highs in the \nupper 80s to lower 90s Thursday and Friday. This could occur if the \nsoutherly flow can reach further north into Southern Nebraska, or if \nsolar heating can develop. Even so, rain is in the forecast, as NBM \n24 hr precipitation guidance shows a 40% chance or greater of \nreceiving more than 0.1 inches of rain both Wednesday and Thursday \nacross most of the forecast area. These probabilities are highest \nalong a line from Norton, KS to Tribune, KS Wednesday (50-70%), and \nin Northeastern Colorado and far Southwestern Nebraska Thursday (60-\n75%). Most of this activity is forecast to be widespread rain rather \nthan localized convective storms, the exception being Wednesday. \nLREF guidance suggests a 40-55% chance across most of the forecast \narea of experiencing surface CAPE (a measure of atmospheric \ninstability) greater than 1000 J/kg Wednesday evening. Some higher \nend scenarios (90th percentile and greater) show CAPE may rise above \n2000 J/kg. Even so, this convective activity is not favored to be \nsevere, as GFS and EC soundings show sfc-6km shear remaining below \n30 kts during the afternoon and evening hours. Confidence in severe \nweather occurring Wednesday is 5% or less. \n\nThe forecast begins to become a bit more uncertain by Saturday \nmorning, specifically regarding the trough to the west of the \nforecast area. This feature is expected to be moving slowly eastward \nthroughout the long term period, though guidance is inconsistent on \nwhen it will move over the forecast area. However, once it moves over\nthe area, temperatures may drop a bit more, in addition to \nprecipitation chances. This is represented in the forecast \ntemperatures for both Saturday and Sunday, with highs in the mid\nto upper 70s. Additionally, NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance \ndrops the probabilities for greater than 0.1 inches of rain \nbelow 40% on Sunday. These probabilities are a little higher on \nSaturday, but is likely due to a higher chance that the trough \nis still west of the forecast area on Saturday. In other words, \nconditions similar to Wednesday through Friday are expected as \nlong as the trough stays to the west of the forecast area. \nHowever, once it moves in over the region, temperatures and \nprecipitation would likely drop off a little bit.",
